.TH HTTPS 2
.SH NAME
https: request \- HTTP/1.1 client over this tree's own TLS
.SH SYNOPSIS
.EX
include "sys.m";
include "keyring.m";
include "tls12.m";
include "https.m";
https := load Https Https->PATH;

init:    fn(): string;
request: fn(method, url: string,
            hdrs: list of (string, string),
            body: array of byte):
            (int, list of (string, string), array of byte, string);
.EE
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B Https
performs one HTTP/1.1 request over TLS 1.2 using
.IR tls12 (2),
which verifies the server's certificate chain against the tree's own
trust roots and refuses the connection otherwise. It exists so
network clients need no host command as a transport;
.IR bsky (1)
and
.IR masto (1)
are its first users.
.PP
.B Init
must be called once and returns nil on success.
.B Request
takes a
.I method
("GET", "POST", ...), an
.I url
of the form
.BR https://\fIhost\fP[:\fIport\fP]/\fIpath\fP?\fIquery\fP ,
extra request headers as
.RI ( name ,\  value )
pairs, and an optional request body
(a
.B Content-Length
header is added automatically when it is non-nil). It returns
.BI ( status ,\  headers ,\  body ,\  err ) .
.I Err
is nil whenever a complete HTTP response came back:
an HTTP error status (4xx/5xx) is a
.I result
whose body is returned like any other \(em APIs put their
diagnostics there \(em not an error. Response header names arrive
lower-cased, in arrival order.
.PP
The response body is read strictly by the response's own framing:
.B Content-Length
when given, the
.B chunked
transfer-coding when declared, and read-to-close otherwise. Each
request uses its own TLS session
.RB ( "Connection: close" );
nothing is reused. Redirects are not followed \(em the status and
.B location
header come back to the caller instead.
.PP
A hostname in the URL is resolved by
.IR doh (2)
\(em DNS over HTTPS, over this same TLS \(em so the whole client
resolves names by itself, needing no
.I /net/cs
and no host resolver. Resolutions are cached for the life of the
process. A literal IP in the URL is dialled directly (no lookup). If
DoH resolution fails, it falls back to dialling the name through a
connection server (\c
.IR ndb/cs (8),
if one serves
.IR /net/cs )
and then the hosted
.IR emu 's
built-in
.B $Srv
resolver.
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/lib/https.b
.br
.B /module/https.m
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR httpstest (1),
.IR tls12 (2),
.IR dial (2),
.IR bsky (1),
.IR masto (1)
.SH BUGS
One request per connection; no keep-alive, no pipelining, no
compression, no redirects, no proxies, and
.B https
only \(em there is deliberately no cleartext
.B http
mode. Everything
.IR tls12 (2)'s
BUGS section says (no revocation checking above all) applies to
every request made through this.
